# <%= module_name %>

A [GemStack](https://github.com/gemstack/gemstack) application: a Ruby API with a
Next.js + TypeScript frontend, served from one origin.

```bash
gemstack dev          # http://localhost:3000  (/api/* and /jobs → Ruby)
gemstack routes       # list API routes
gemstack test         # run the Ruby tests
gemstack console      # IRB with the app loaded
gemstack generate controller Status show
```

## Layout

| Path | What lives there |
|---|---|
| `config/app.rb` | configuration (`GemStack.configure`) |
| `config/routes.rb` | API routes, relative to `/api` |
| `app/` | autoloaded code: `controllers/`, and any folder you add (`services/`, ...) |
| `test/` | Minitest + rack-test (`GemStack::TestCase`) |
| `frontend/` | the Next.js app; `lib/gemstack/client.ts` talks to the API |

## Background jobs

Database-backed apps include the jobs migration and the built-in dashboard at
`/jobs`. In development, `gemstack dev` sends `/jobs` to Ruby and starts the
queue worker after the migration is present. The dashboard can inspect queue
state and completed-job history, and retry or discard failed jobs.

The dashboard uses the `:database` job adapter. Other adapters do not expose
the persisted queue data it needs. In production, configure
`config.jobs.dashboard.authorization` in `config/app.rb` to authorize admin
requests. The callback must return exactly `true` for an authorized admin;
without one the dashboard returns 404. Check the production setup with
`gemstack doctor --production`, or disable the dashboard with
`config.jobs.dashboard.enabled = false`. If your reverse proxy routes traffic
directly, send both the API path and the configured dashboard path to the Ruby
server.

For an existing released-gem app, run `gemstack update` to update GemStack and
reconcile generated files. Review any template conflicts and keep
`require "gemstack/jobs"` in `config/app.rb`. Template updates do not create or
apply migrations: if the app has no jobs migration, run `gemstack jobs:install`
and `gemstack db:migrate`. Apps using GemStack from a `path` checkout must
update that checkout first.

## Production

Run the API with `gemstack server` (Puma, `config/puma.rb`) and the frontend
with `npm run build && npm start` in `frontend/`. Either route `/api/*` to the
API with your reverse proxy, or set `GEMSTACK_API_URL` for the Next.js process
and it will proxy `/api/*` itself. When enabled, route `/jobs` and `/jobs/*` to
the Ruby server too; configure authorization before exposing it.
